        "problem": "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(4+x)^{4}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.05)^{4}.$ Give your answer to three decimal places.",
        "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+\u2026+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=4,$ $b=x,$ and $n=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(4+x)}^{4} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(4)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(4)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(4)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1{(4)}^{4}+4{(4)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(4)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =256+4(64)(x)+6(16)({x}^{2})+\u22ef \\\\  & =256+256x+96{x}^{2}+\u22ef\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n(4+x)^{4}\u2248256+256x+96{x}^{2}+\u22ef\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.05$ into the expansion of $(4+x)^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4.05)^{4} & =(4+0.05)^{4} \\\\  & \u2248256+256(0.05)+96(0.05)^{2} \\\\  & =256+256(0.05)+96(0.002\\,5) \\\\  & =256+12.8+0.24 \\\\  & =269.040,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places.",
